  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use a combination of structured initialization, local optimization, and dynamic constraint enforcement to find a better solution than the existing parent. This approach leverages the observation that the Erd\u0151s problem benefits from balancing the function's mass across the interval while minimizing overlap at critical lags. By using a carefully constructed initial guess with periodicity and symmetry, we avoid local optima that plague gradient-based methods. The key limitation is computational feasibility, which is addressed through adaptive sampling and efficient constraint handling.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points: step function with values in [0,1]\n- dx = 2.0 / n_points: grid spacing\n- target_sum = n_points / 2: required integral constraint\n- C\u2085 = max_k [\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx] * dx (exact objective)\n\nConstraints:\n1. \u2211h[i] = target_sum\n2. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n\nObjective: Minimize C\u2085\n\nImplementation details:\n- The exact objective is computed via np.correlate with 'full' mode and proper scaling\n- The grid is sampled at n_points points with dx = 2.0 / n_points\n- The function's integral is enforced via scaling after each update\n- Constraints are maintained using Lagrange multipliers in optimization\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nParent validation:\n- If initial_h_values provided, normalize it to [0,1]^n_points\n- Check \u2211h \u2248 target_sum within 1e-6\n\nInitialization:\n- For n_points=400, create an initial guess h_initial with a periodic structure\n  h_initial = np.sin(np.pi * x / 2) + 1.0\n  h_initial = h_initial / np.max(h_initial) * 0.5\n  h_initial = h_initial * (target_sum / np.sum(h_initial))\n- If no parent provided, generate a random initial guess with uniform distribution and normalization\n\nOptimization loop:\nFor each iteration (up to 600s):\na) Compute exact overlaps using np.correlate\nb) Identify the worst k lag with max_overlap\nc) Apply a local descent on the max_overlap k using gradient-based optimization\nd) Use L-BFGS-B with bounds [0,1] and constraint \u2211h = target_sum\ne) After each descent, apply soft clipping and rescaling to maintain bounds and constraint\nf) Track best solution found so far (lowest C\u2085)\n\nDynamic sampling:\n- After 30% of budget, switch to gradient-based refinement with adaptive step sizes\n- After 50% of budget, perform random perturbation on top solution with 0.08 magnitude\n- After 70% of budget, perform final refinement using constrained optimization with tight tolerances\n\nConstraint handling:\n- Enforce \u2211h = target_sum via Lagrangian relaxation in optimization\n- Apply soft clipping with penalty term when violating bounds\n- Normalize after each update to preserve integral constraint\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault Configuration:\n- n_points=400 (balances resolution and time)\n- Initial period=2.0 (full interval)\n- Sampling frequency=400 points\n- Iteration limit=600s\n- Initial perturbation magnitude=0.08\n- Refinement iterations=30\n- Soft clipping penalty=100.0\n\nVariations:\n- Adaptive sampling: Increase perturbation magnitude when C\u2085 stagnates\n- Multi-frequency: Use sinusoidal functions with different periods\n- Adaptive constraints: Adjust penalty terms based on progress\n- Hybrid refinement: Combine L-BFGS-B with projected gradient descent\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime Allocation:\n- 150s: Initial structured optimization\n- 200s: Gradient refinement with adaptive steps\n- 200s: Random exploration and final refinement\n\nRobustness Measures:\n- Maintain valid solutions via normalization and clipping\n- Track best solution after every iteration\n- Use exponential decay for perturbation magnitude\n- Limit individual update complexity to O(n_points)\n- Store parent solution as fallback\n\nValidation:\n- Re-compute C\u2085 using exact objective function\n- Ensure results match evaluator's calculation\n- Perform sanity checks:\n   a) Correlation length matches 2*n_points-1\n   b) Overlap values are positive\n   c) Maximum overlap is correctly identified\n   d) Constraint satisfaction is within tolerance\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility Checks:\n- \u2211h \u2248 target_sum within 1e-6\n- All h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n- Correlation calculation uses mode=\"full\" with proper scaling\n\nFinal Validation:\n- Re-compute C\u2085 using exact objective function\n- Ensure results match evaluator's calculation\n- Perform sanity checks:\n   a) Correlation length matches expected size\n   b) Overlap values are positive\n   c) Maximum overlap is correctly identified\n   d) Constraint satisfaction is within tolerance\n\nReturn Format:\n- h_values as list of floats in [0,1]\n- c5_bound as exact value\n- n_points as integer",
